etypeAndExt function, specifically the lack of a dangerous-extension denylist and the override of WordPress's built-in defenses. Authenticated attackers with custom-level access or ...\n [link] https://vulmon.com/vulnerabilitydetails?qid=CVE-2026-109384\n [webpage 1 end]\n[webpage 2 begin] [title] CVE-2026-109384: Melograno Unrestricted File Upload | CVETodo\n [description] CVE-2026-109384: Unrestricted File Upload in The Innovative Form Builder IvyForms. Includes technical details, affected versions, and mitigation steps.\n [link] https://cvetodo.com/cve/CVE-2026-109384\n [webpage 2 end]\n[webpage 3 begin] [title] CVE-2026-109384 - The Innovative Form Builder <= 1.5.1 - Authenticated ...\n [description] The following list is the news that have been mention CVE-2026-109384 vulnerability anywhere in the article. Results are limited to the first 20 news articles due to potential performance issues. EPSS is a daily estimate of the probability of exploitation activity being observed over the next 30 days.\n [link] https://cvefeed.io/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-109384\n [webpage 3 end]\n[webpage 4 begin] [title] CVE-2026-109384: melograno The Innovative Form Builder - Rapid7\n [description] CVE-2026-109384: melograno The Innovative Form Builder - IvyForms: The The Innovative Form Builder - IvyForms plugin for WordPress is vulnerable to Arbitrary File Upload in all versions…. View severity, references, and remediation details from Rapid7.\n [link] https://www.rapid7.com/db/vulnerabilities/cve-2026-109384/\n [webpage 4 end]\n[webpage 5 begin] [title] CVE-2026-109384: Remote Code Execution (RCE ...\n [description] 2 days ago · [No Public PoC] CVE-2026-109384 (Remote Code Execution (RCE)) is a High severity flaw (CVSS 8.8). The The Innovative Form Builder – IvyForms plugin for WordPress is vulnerable to Arbitrary File Upload... Technical threat analysis, CVSS breakdown, and vendor patch guidance.\n [link] https://www.thehackerwire.com/vulnerability/CVE-2026-109384/\n [webpage 5 end]\n[webpage 6 begin] [title] CVE-2026-109384 · WordPress · CVSS 8.8 · CVE Brief Analyst Report\n [description] 2 days ago · This vulnerability poses a severe risk to the integrity and availability of the WordPress environment. Successful exploitation allows an attacker to upload executable files, granting them the ability to execute arbitrary code on the server, which may lead to full system compromise, data theft, or the deployment of persistent backdoors.\n [link] https://cvebrief.com/cve/cve-2026-109384/\n [webpage 6 end]\n\n            \n        ```\n    *   **漏洞利用代码：**\n        ```\n        /tmp/7895eb5f0f68c615c7ebf70572b03384/README.md\n---\n# CVE-2026-109384 — IvyForms <= 1.5.1\n\n**Unauthenticated arbitrary file upload leading to remote code execution (RCE)**\n\n> **Author:** NØXCRYPT · [@noxcrypt-x](https://github.com/noxcrypt-x)\n>\n> **Disclaimer:** This repository is provided for security research and authorized testing only. Do not use it against systems you do not own or have explicit permission to test. The author is not responsible for any misuse.\n>\n> **Repository contents:** PoC script and documentation only. The local lab (`lab/`) and plugin sources (`src/`, `src152/`) are not included to keep the repository small.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Product | The Innovative Form Builder – IvyForms (WordPress plugin) |\n| Affected versions | All versions up to 1.5.1 |\n| Fixed in | 1.5.2 (released October 21, 2026) |\n| Class | CWE-434 — Unrestricted Upload of File with Dangerous Type |\n| CVSS 3.1 | 8.8 High — `AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H` |\n| Source | Wordfence (CNA), published October 27–28, 2026 |\n| Identifiers | CVE-2026-109384 / GHSA-qrwx-hpgq-g68q |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Summary\n\nIvyForms lets a form owner — or any user holding the `ivyforms_add_edit_forms` capability, which the plugin exposes as a \"custom\" access level — choose the allowed file extensions for a `file-upload` field. The extension sanitizer has no denylist, so it accepts `php`, `phtml`, `phar`, and similar script extensions.\n\nOn submission, the plugin registers a filter on `wp_check_filetype_and_ext` that forces the allow-listed extension and MIME type, bypassing WordPress's native extension and MIME checks. `wp_handle_upload()` then stores the file under:\n\n```text\nwp-content/uploads/ivyforms/entry/<name>.php\n```\n\nThe `/wp-json/ivyforms/v1/form/submission/` endpoint is public (`permission_callback => '__return_true'`), and the two nonces it requires are printed into the HTML of any page that renders the form. Once the form is configured, an unauthenticated visitor can replay the upload.\n\n**Impact:** webshell upload and execution → full server compromise.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Root Cause\n\n### 2.1 Missing denylist in the extension sanitizer\n\n`backend/src/Common/Sanitizer/Sanitizer.php` — `sanitizeAllowedFileExtensionsInput()`:\n\n```php\n$ext = strtolower(ltrim(trim($item), '.'));\nif ($ext === '' || strlen($ext) > 20 || !preg_match('/^[a-z0-9]+$/', $ext)) {\n    continue;\n}\n```\n\nThe check accepts any short alphanumeric string, including `php`, `phtml`, `phar`, `html`, and `svg`. There is no dangerous-extension denylist.\n\n### 2.2 WordPress MIME and extension check bypass\n\n`backend/src/Services/Submission/SubmissionUploadFiletypeFilter.php`:\n\n```php\n$ext = self::resolveExtensionForFilter($filename);\nif ($ext === '' || !self::isExtensionPermitted($ext)) {\n    return $data;\n}\n\nreturn self::buildAcceptedFiletype($ext, $mimes);\n// ['ext' => 'php', 'type' => 'application/octet-stream', 'proper_filename' => false]\n```\n\nThe filter is registered in `SubmissionUploadFilePersister::storeUploadedFile()` on `wp_check_filetype_and_ext`. WordPress therefore receives `ext=php` and `type=application/octet-stream` and accepts the file instead of rejecting it.\n\n### 2.3 Public endpoint with exposed nonces\n\n`backend/src/Routes/Form/Form.php`:\n\n```php\nregister_rest_route(\n    $routeNamespace,\n    '/form/submission/',\n    [\n        'methods'             => WP_REST_Server::CREATABLE,\n        'callback'            => $container->get(FormSubmissionController::class),\n        'permission_callback' => '__return_true'\n    ]\n);\n```\n\n`FormSubmissionController::handle()` only validates:\n\n- `X-WP-Nonce` in the `wp_rest` format\n- `nonce` in the `ivyformsFrontSubmissionNonce_<formId>` format\n\nBoth are generated for anonymous visitors and printed into the page by `wp_localize_script()`:\n\n```js\nvar wpIvyApiSettings = {\"root\":\"...\",\"nonce\":\"...\",\"namespace\":\"ivyforms/v1\"};\nvar wpIvyFormDataList = {\"<formId>_<uniqid>\":{\"form\":{...},\"nonce\":\"...\",\"fields\":[...]}};\n```\n\n### 2.4 Upload path\n\n`SubmissionUploadDirectoryFilter` changes the upload directory to `/ivyforms/entry` (without year/month subdirectories), and `wp_handle_upload()` preserves the original filename. Version 1.5.1 does not ship an `.htaccess` file that blocks execution there.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Exploit Chain\n\n### Path A — low-privileged user (custom access)\n\n1. Log in as an account with `ivyforms_add_edit_forms` (for example, a Subscriber plus an IvyForms access rule).\n2. Create a published form containing a `file-upload` field with `allowedFileExtensions: [\"php\",\"phtml\",...]`.\n3. Open the form preview: `/?ivyforms_preview=<id>&_wpNonce=<wp_rest nonce>` — available to any user with `add_edit_forms`.\n4. Extract the nonces from the preview page.\n5. Send a `multipart/form-data` request to `/wp-json/ivyforms/v1/form/submission/` with a PHP file.\n6. The file is stored and publicly executed at `/wp-content/uploads/ivyforms/entry/shell.php`.\n\nThis path escalates a regular user to RCE without administrator involvement.\n\n### Path B — unauthenti\n        ```\n\n    **你的角色：** 你是一名专业的网络安全分析师，擅长分析CVE漏洞信息。\n\n    **任务：**\n\n    1.  **CVE有效性：** 分析此CVE是否为真实存在的有效漏洞。请综合考虑CVE编号格式、漏洞描述的完整性、参考链接的存在、漏洞的技术细节等因素。\n    2.  **POC有效性：** 判断提供的POC代码是否有效，是否能实际利用此漏洞，并分析其有效性。\n    3.  **投毒风险：** 分析POC代码内容,判断此仓库中是否